Swap provides on-disk memory that the system will use if it runs out of
read-access memory at any point.

zram on the other hand creates a compressed block device in RAM on-the-fly. If
the system runs out of memory at any point, the zram blocked device will be
created and existing in-memory data will be moved to it, thus freeing up space
in RAM.

On modern systems with a RAM sizes of 16GB and more, the system can run without
any issues using only zram and no swap space at all. Swap can still be used as a
safety net or if you have a specific RAM-heavy use case in mind. Furthermore, if
you want to use hibernation on your system, swap space at least the size of the
available RAM must be set up.

If you decide to use swap, a swap file will be used. Unlike a swap partition, a
swap file's size can be adapted much easier later if desired.
